c2.append(P("What it is not", h2))
c2.append(P("NEET is not an aptitude test. It is a syllabus-coverage test with negative "
            "marking, built almost entirely on NCERT content. It rewards candidates "
            "who have read the same books repeatedly and practised under a clock, not "
            "candidates who own the most expensive coaching material. The 2026 cycle "
            "proved the point again: of the 138 candidates who scored above 690, more "
            "than 93 percent were appearing for the first time, and 99 percent were "
            "between 17 and 19 years old — ordinary students on an ordinary timeline "
            "who executed well."))
c2.append(mk_table(
    ["Fact", "Detail (verified for the 2026 cycle)"],
    [["Conducting body", "National Testing Agency (NTA)"],
     ["Syllabus authority", "National Medical Commission (NMC)"],
     ["Courses covered", "MBBS, BDS, BAMS, BUMS, BSMS, BHMS, BVSc and AH, B.Sc. Nursing (incl. MNS)"],
     ["Official website", "neet.nta.nic.in (results also on nta.ac.in)"],
     ["Frequency", "Once a year"],
     ["Mode (2026)", "Pen-and-paper, single day, single shift (2:00 to 5:00 PM IST)"]],
    [0.30, 0.70]))
S += chapter(2, "What NEET Actually Is", c2)

# ============================================================== CH 3
c3 = []
c3.append(P("The 2026 cycle was the most turbulent in NEET's history, and every "
            "aspirant starting now should understand what happened — not for the "
            "drama, but for what it teaches about building a preparation that "
            "survives surprises.", lead))
c3.append(P("The timeline, in brief. NEET (UG) 2026 was first held on 3 May 2026, "
            "after a registration window that ran from 8 February to 8 March. On "
            "12 May, NTA cancelled the result after allegations that the question "
            "paper had been leaked — an email had pointed out overlaps between a "
            "circulated guess paper and the actual 3 May paper. A re-examination was "
            "held on 21 June 2026 across 5,440 centres in 551 cities in India and "
            "14 cities abroad. Results were declared on 16 July 2026, in time to keep "
            "the medical admission calendar on track. The CBI took over the leak "
            "investigation and made arrests through the year."))
c3.append(P("Three lessons for your 2027 season:"))
c3.append(numbered([
    "Depth beats format. Whether the paper is printed or on a screen, administered in May or June, NCERT mastery, formula recall and timed MCQ practice are what convert to marks. Prepare the content, and the format takes care of itself.",
    "Do not build your season around rumours. In 2026, fake 'leaked papers', guessed answer keys and reschedule rumours consumed more aspirant hours than any chapter of Physics. Official notices come from one place: neet.nta.nic.in.",
    "The system recovers, and so can you. A cancelled paper and a six-week delay still ended in a completed counselling calendar. Candidates who kept a steady daily rhythm through the chaos were the ones who were ready on 21 June.",
]))
c3.append(Spacer(1, 4))
c3.append(callout("A NOTE ON THE 2027 MODE",
                  "The 2026 exam was pen-and-paper on OMR sheets. Following the leak, "
                  "media reports suggested the exam could move to computer-based "
                  "testing in future cycles. Nothing had been notified as of September "
                  "2026. Treat mode as unknown: if you practise on paper, also take a "
                  "few online mocks each month so a screen feels ordinary either way."))
S += chapter(3, "The 2026 Lesson: A Cancelled Paper and a Re-Exam", c3)
S.append(PageBreak())

# ============================================================== CH 4
c4 = []
c4.append(P("The exam machine is simple, published, and identical for every candidate. "
            "Learn it once, properly, and it stops being a source of exam-day anxiety.", lead))
c4.append(P("The structure of the paper", h2))
c4.append(mk_table(
    ["Subject", "Questions", "Marks", "Share of paper"],
    [["Physics", "45", "180", "25%"],
     ["Chemistry", "45", "180", "25%"],
     ["Biology (Botany + Zoology)", "90", "360", "50%"],
     ["Total", "180", "720", "100%"]],
    [0.40, 0.20, 0.20, 0.20]))
c4.append(P("All 180 questions are compulsory. There is no optional-question buffer "
            "and no internal choice: every candidate answers the same paper. The exam "
            "runs for 180 minutes — 2:00 PM to 5:00 PM IST — which is exactly one "
            "minute per question, and less than that once you account for OMR "
            "shading and review."))
c4.append(P("Marking, exactly as NTA states it", h2))
c4.append(bullets([
    "Correct answer or the most appropriate answer: +4 marks.",
    "Any incorrect option marked: -1 mark.",
    "Unattempted question: 0 marks.",
    "If more than one option is found correct after review, +4 is awarded to everyone who marked any of the correct options.",
    "If all options are found correct, +4 goes to every candidate who attempted the question.",
    "If a question is wrong, dropped, or has no correct option, +4 is awarded to all candidates who appeared — attempted or not.",
]))
c4.append(P("The last three rules matter more than they look. They mean you should "
            "never leave a question blank purely from fear of an incorrect key — but "
            "blind guessing is still a losing bet (Chapter 22 shows the arithmetic). "
            "They also mean dropped questions are a gift to everyone equally, so do "
            "not build a strategy around them."))
c4.append(P("Language and paper", h2))
c4.append(P("The 2026 paper was offered in 13 languages: Assamese, Bengali, English, "
            "Gujarati, Hindi, Kannada, Malayalam, Marathi, Odia, Punjabi, Tamil, "
            "Telugu and Urdu. In 2026, over 18 lakh of the 22.8 lakh registered "
            "candidates chose English and about 3.45 lakh chose Hindi. Pick the "
            "language you think in, not the one that sounds more serious — the "
            "translation of a Biology question you misread is still a misread."))
c4.append(P("The NEET syllabus is notified by the NMC and is built on the NCERT "
            "Class 11 and 12 syllabus. The 2026 Information Bulletin carried the "
            "detailed syllabus as Appendix-III, available on the NTA and NMC "
            "websites. Chapter 9 sorts it for you."))
S += chapter(4, "The Exam, Question by Question", c4)

# ============================================================== CH 5
c5 = []
c5.append(P("Nothing for 2027 has been notified yet — and that is normal. NTA has "
            "announced NEET dates in the winter before the exam for years. What you "
            "can do now is learn the shape of the calendar from the 2026 cycle, so "
            "that when the 2027 Information Bulletin drops, you recognise every page "
            "of it.", lead))
c5.append(mk_table(
    ["Event", "2026 (actual)", "2027 (expected)"],
    [["Information Bulletin / notification", "8 February 2026", "February 2027"],
     ["Registration window", "8 Feb - 8 March (fee to 11:50 PM)", "About 4 weeks, Feb - March"],
     ["Correction window", "10 - 12 March", "A few days after closing"],
     ["City intimation slip", "April", "April"],
     ["Admit card", "April (about 2 weeks before)", "April 2027"],
     ["Exam day", "3 May (Sunday); re-exam 21 June", "First or second Sunday of May 2027"],
     ["Provisional answer key and challenges", "Within days of the exam", "May - June"],
     ["Result", "16 July (re-exam cycle)", "June 2027"],
     ["Counselling (MCC AIQ) begins", "5 August", "July - August 2027"]],
    [0.34, 0.33, 0.33]))
c5.append(Spacer(1, 4))
c5.append(P("The most likely 2027 exam date is the first Sunday of May — 2 May 2027 — "
            "based on the pattern of recent years (5 May 2024, 4 May 2025, 3 May 2026). "
            "Treat that as a planning anchor, not a promise; NTA confirms dates only "
            "in the official notification."))
c5.append(callout("WHAT TO DO WITH THIS TABLE NOW",
                  "Set one recurring reminder: check neet.nta.nic.in in the first week "
                  "of February 2027. Missing the registration window has no late-fee "
                  "rescue — it means waiting for 2028. Everything else in the table "
                  "can be handled calmly after the bulletin arrives."))
c5.append(P("And keep your documents ready before the window opens, not during it: "
            "a recent passport photograph, a scanned signature, address proof, and "
            "category certificate if you claim one. Chapter 21 has the full "
            "registration walkthrough."))
S += chapter(5, "NEET 2027: The Expected Timeline", c5)
S.append(PageBreak())

# ============================================================== CH 6
c6 = []
c6.append(P("NEET's eligibility rules are short, and every one of them has ended "
            "candidacies when discovered late. Read this chapter twice — once now, "
            "once in January.", lead))
c6.append(mk_table(
    ["Rule", "What it means"],
    [["Age", "You must turn 17 by 31 December of the admission year. For NEET 2027, that means born on or before 31 December 2010."],
     ["Subjects in Class 12", "Physics, Chemistry and Biology / Biotechnology, with English. The 2026 cycle allowed PCB even as additional subjects, with documentary proof at counselling."],
     ["Minimum aggregate in PCB", "50% for unreserved; 40% for SC / ST / OBC; 45% for unreserved PwBD — in Physics, Chemistry and Biology / Biotechnology combined."],
     ["Attempts", "There is no cap on the number of attempts. Candidates may appear any number of years."],
     ["Nationality", "Indian nationals, NRIs, OCIs, PIOs and foreign nationals are all eligible; some seat categories (like NRI-quota seats) have their own rules."],
     ["Qualifying-exam codes", "The bulletin assigns codes 01 to 07 to the kind of Class 12 / equivalent qualification you hold. Code 02 is the standard Indian Class 12 with PCB."]],
    [0.28, 0.72]))
c6.append(P("The 50/40/45 rule is the quiet trap for droppers and for students who "
            "treated Boards casually in their first pass. It is checked at admission, "
            "not at registration — a NEET score without the Class 12 aggregate is a "
            "score that cannot be cashed. If your board marks are the weak link, "
            "Chapter 19 shows how to protect them without derailing NEET prep."))
c6.append(P("One more check that belongs in September, not March: if you plan to claim "
            "a category (OBC-NCL, EWS, SC, ST or PwBD), verify that the certificate "
            "exists, is in the prescribed format, and is in the name of the person "
            "filling the form. Category claims that fail document verification at "
            "counselling are treated as unreserved — and lost seats."))
S += chapter(6, "Who Can Sit: Eligibility Decoded", c6)

# ============================================================== CH 7
c7 = []
c7.append(P("You cannot set a target score without knowing the field. Here is the "
            "field, from NTA's own result statistics for 2026.", lead))
c7.append(mk_table(
    ["Year", "Registered", "Appeared", "Qualified"],
    [["2023", "20,87,462", "20,38,596", "11,45,932"],
     ["2024", "24,06,079", "23,33,297", "13,16,268"],
     ["2025", "22,76,069", "22,09,318", "12,36,531"],
     ["2026", "22,79,743", "19,99,895", "11,21,185"]],
    [0.16, 0.28, 0.28, 0.28]))
c7.append(P("In 2026, 11.21 lakh candidates qualified — about 56 percent of those "
            "who appeared — and the highest score was 715 out of 720, shared by two "
            "candidates. Just 19 candidates crossed 700; 1,492 crossed 650; 10,160 "
            "crossed 600; and 90,780 crossed 500. Those numbers should recalibrate "
            "every 'just need 650' plan you have heard: 650 put you among the top "
            "1,500 candidates in the country in 2026."))
c7.append(P("Qualifying vs getting a seat", h2))
c7.append(P("NTA sets qualifying cutoffs as percentiles, not marks. In 2026 the "
            "unreserved / EWS cutoff was the 50th percentile — a range of 213 to 715 "
            "marks — while OBC, SC and ST candidates needed the 40th percentile, 177 "
            "marks and above. For perspective, in 2025 the unreserved range started "
            "at 144 marks, and in 2024 at 162: the qualifying line moves with the "
            "paper's difficulty and the cohort, so never plan to 'the cutoff'."))
c7.append(P("Qualifying, however, only makes you eligible for counselling. Seats go "
            "at much higher scores. With roughly 1.37 lakh MBBS seats nationally "
            "(Chapter 8) and 11.2 lakh qualified candidates, the median qualifier "
            "does not get a government MBBS seat. Realistic bands from recent "
            "counselling cycles:"))
c7.append(bullets([
    "670+ competes for the top government colleges through All India Quota.",
    "620 - 670 is competitive for government MBBS in most states, depending on domicile and category.",
    "560 - 620 keeps government MBBS in reach in several states and for reserved categories, and opens government BDS and AYUSH more widely.",
    "Below 500, the realistic pool is private colleges (with their fees), deemed universities, and allied courses — decide deliberately, not in the last round of counselling.",
]))
c7.append(P("One number that should genuinely encourage you: in 2026, women both "
            "outnumbered men among qualifiers (58 percent) and qualified at a higher "
            "rate (56.8 vs 55.1 percent). And of the 138 candidates above 690 marks, "
            "99 percent were 17 to 19 years old — this exam is won by students your "
            "age on their first attempt, not by an unseen army of veterans."))
S += chapter(7, "The Numbers That Define the Race", c7)
S.append(PageBreak())

# ============================================================== CH 8
c8 = []
c8.append(P("For 2026-27, the NMC's seat matrix counted 1,36,939 MBBS seats across "
            "823 medical colleges (excluding the Institutes of National Importance, "
            "admitted separately through the same NEET score), with 9,911 new seats "
            "added that year. Add BDS, AYUSH, nursing and veterinary seats and the "
            "total is larger still — but MBBS at a government college remains the "
            "prize most of the 20-lakh-strong field is running for.", lead))
c8.append(P("How those seats are divided", h2))
c8.append(P("Every government medical college contributes 15 percent of its seats to "
            "the All India Quota (AIQ). The remaining 85 percent — plus private "
            "college seats in that state — are filled by the state's own counselling "
            "authority. On top of the AIQ, the Medical Counselling Committee (MCC) of "
            "the DGHS handles 100 percent of seats at AIIMS institutions, JIPMER, "
            "deemed universities, and central universities like BHU, AMU and Delhi "
            "University colleges, plus ESIC and AFMC registration."))
c8.append(mk_table(
    ["Track", "Who runs it", "What it covers"],
    [["AIQ", "MCC (mcc.nic.in)", "15% of govt college seats, all India, no domicile bar"],
     ["Central / deemed", "MCC", "100% AIIMS, JIPMER, BHU, AMU, DU colleges, deemed universities, ESIC"],
     ["State quota", "State authorities", "85% of govt seats + private colleges, domicile rules apply"]],
    [0.22, 0.22, 0.56]))
c8.append(P("Reservation in the AIQ follows the central policy: 15 percent SC, 7.5 "
            "percent ST, 27 percent OBC (non-creamy layer), 10 percent EWS, and 5 "
            "percent horizontal PwBD. States run their own — often larger — "
            "reservation lists for the 85 percent quota, which is one reason your "
            "domicile state matters as much as your score band."))
c8.append(P("The counselling sequence", h2))
c8.append(P("MCC counselling runs in rounds — Round 1, Round 2, a Mop-Up round for "
            "the still-unallotted, and a Stray Vacancy round. In Round 1 you get a "
            "'free exit': you can walk away from an allotted seat with your security "
            "deposit refunded. After Round 2, exiting costs the deposit — Rs. 10,000 "
            "for the AIQ track (Rs. 5,000 for SC / ST / OBC / PwBD) and Rs. 2,00,000 "
            "for deemed universities. Register separately on your state portal too: "
            "most candidates do both, and state quota is where the majority of seats "
            "live."))
c8.append(callout("FEE REALITY CHECK",
                  "Government college MBBS fees are a few thousand rupees a year in "
